A released view (zombie, 0108) let go of its surface object.

The zombie only needs its mapped state page, which holds the state memory
by itself. Keeping the surface retained kept up to 256 screen surfaces
(13.9 MB each) alive, filled SWEET_SURFACE_WIRED_MAX and made every new
backboardd surface fail with kIOReturnNoMemory (all4: the Wi-Fi settings
freeze, 1872 failed creates).

--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetSurface.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetSurface.cpp
@@ -1500,9 +1500,13 @@
 		 * The pixels are unmapped now; the read-only state page stays mapped a while
 		 * (Topaz 0137): another IOSurface object of the task may still read its seed.
 		 * The last 256 released views keep their page, the oldest goes first.
+		 * The page's map holds the state memory by itself, so the view lets go of
+		 * the surface: a zombie kept a 13.9 MB screen surface alive, and 256 of
+		 * them filled the wired budget until every new frame failed (all4).
 		 */
 		OSSafeReleaseNULL(ref->data);
 		sweet_surface_put(ref->surface);
+		OSSafeReleaseNULL(ref->surface);
 		ref->released = true;
 		ref->detached = false;
 		if (fZombies->setObject(ref) && fZombies->getCount() > 256) {
